course: dictionary
lesson: 14695
title: Previous: the one before
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: «Previous» tells you about something that came before the one you are talking about now. Look at the words around it to find what it is being compared to. «Turn to the previous page.» The word page is the clue: you are going back to the page before this one.
explain: In a number pattern, the previous number is the one just before. In 3, 6, 9, 12, the number previous to 12 is 9, because 9 comes right before 12 in the pattern.
explain: Check the meaning by swapping in «the one before». «The previous day» becomes «the day before», and if it still makes sense, you have it right. Being «previous» in the informal sense is different, and you will find it in the facts below.
fact: Previous means happening or existing before now, or before the one being talked about.
fact: «Previous» often goes before a noun: the previous day, the previous week, the previous owner.
fact: «Previous to» is a formal way of saying before, as in «previous to the match».
fact: «No previous experience» means you have never done something before.
fact: In informal UK English, «You're a bit previous!» means you are too quick or eager, and have acted before it is time.
exercise: «Mia read the previous chapter before bed.» What does previous mean here? | The most exciting one | The last one in the whole book | *The one just before | The one just after
exercise: In the pattern 3, 6, 9, 12, what is the previous number to 12? | 15 | 3 | *9 | 6
exercise: The job advert says «no previous experience needed.» What does this mean? | You must have done the job before | You must be very old | *You do not need to have done the job before | You need to write a long letter
test: «The previous owner of the house left a red door.» Who owned the house before? | The person who painted the door blue | *The person who owned it before the current owner | The person who is building it next week | The person who lives next door
test: Your friend shouts, «You're a bit previous!» as you start running before the whistle. What does it mean? | *You are too quick, acting before it is time | You have a very kind heart | You are the winner of the race | You are very slow and late
exam: In «previous to the match», what does previous to mean? | During the match | *Before the match | Instead of the match | After the match
